Citation Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Min Cai, Xi Ye, Zhaohui Xu. "EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents." arXiv:2610.16523 (2026). https://pith.science/paper/EBSLUJDR

### The paper claims

Co-evolving the training system with the policy substantially improves multi-turn RL for user-centric agents.

### The reading

Training large language model agents to interact with users over multiple turns is hard because the learning signal depends on the user simulator and environment, which are usually fixed and can be noisy, inconsistent, or uninformative. The paper argues that instead of only improving the policy's reward signal, one should also adapt the simulator, environment, and training configuration during training. It introduces EAU, a meta-agent framework that monitors ongoing RL training, diagnoses failures, and proposes targeted modifications to these components, which are then hot-swapped into the live run. Across three benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving overall scores by 14.7–15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%.

### What carries the argument

EAU (Environment-Agent-User Co-Evolution) — a meta-agent framework where a frontier coding agent acts as a meta-controller that continuously monitors RL training, diagnoses issues in the user simulator, environment, and training dynamics, and proposes programmatic modifications via editable symbolic interfaces. The symbolic user simulator provides deterministic control over information disclosure, mixed with an LLM simulator via an epsilon-greedy strategy. Interventions occur both intra-run (hot-swapped without restart) and inter-run (carried across training runs).

Extended reading notes

### Core claim

The paper shows that co-evolving the user simulator, environment, and training configuration alongside the policy, mediated by a meta-agent, leads to substantial performance gains in multi-turn RL for user-centric agents. The meta-agent observes training trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and statistics; proposes modifications to the user simulator (including a symbolic user with tunable rules), reward design, episode limits, and RL hyperparameters; and validates and hot-swaps these changes into the ongoing training loop. This enables the training system itself to adapt as the policy improves, overcoming instabilities from fixed simulators and environments.

### Load-bearing premise

The meta-agent (a frontier coding model) can reliably diagnose training problems from logs and propose beneficial modifications, even though the integrity gates only constrain writes and do not guarantee improvement.

Editorial extensions

### If this is right

  * If EAU is sound, the standard practice of fixing the simulator and environment during interactive-agent RL training should be replaced by adaptive co-evolution.
  * Meta-agent-guided adaptation could reduce the manual effort of debugging simulators, reward functions, and hyperparameters for multi-turn agent tasks.
  * The observed gains (up to 36.7% on ColBench) suggest that training-system adaptation may be as important as policy optimization in achieving high performance.
  * The method is compatible with multiple RL algorithms (PPO, UserRL, InfoPO) and meta-agent backbones (Claude Opus, Codex), indicating broad applicability.
  * Performance improvements transfer to held-out environments (e.g., Inten. in UserGym), suggesting that co-evolution fosters generalizable agent behaviors.

